---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import GpuCostCalculator from '../../components/GpuCostCalculator.jsx';
---

<ToolLayout
  title="GPU Cost Calculator for Self-Hosting LLMs"
  description="Estimate cloud GPU cost (A100, H100, L40S, RTX 4090) to self-host an open-source LLM — plus VRAM sizing guidance and the real self-host vs. API breakeven point."
>
  <GpuCostCalculator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Self-hosting vs. API: the real cost question</h2>
  <p>
    Self-hosting an open-source model can be cheaper than API calls at high,
    sustained volume — but the GPU runs whether or not you're sending
    requests, unlike pay-per-token API pricing. This calculator helps you
    see the raw infrastructure cost so you can compare it against API
    pricing for your actual usage pattern using the <a href="/tools/api-pricing-calculator">API Pricing Calculator</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    As a rough starting point, the crossover between renting a GPU and paying
    per token tends to land somewhere around <strong>6-10 hours of daily
    use</strong> — below that, API pricing usually wins since you're not
    paying for idle GPU time; above it, a rented or owned GPU usually starts
    winning. Set "Hours/day" to that range in the calculator above and
    compare the total against your expected API spend for the same volume.
  </p>

  <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Estimate how many hours per day your GPU(s) will run.</li>
    <li>Enter the number of days (e.g. 30 for a monthly estimate).</li>
    <li>Enter how many GPUs you'd need for your model size and throughput.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How much VRAM does your model actually need?</h2>
  <p>
    Before picking a GPU count, work out whether the model even fits on one.
    A rough rule of thumb: a model needs about <strong>2 bytes per parameter
    at FP16</strong>, roughly half that at INT8, and roughly a quarter at
    INT4 — plus extra headroom for the context window and KV cache, which
    grows with longer prompts and larger batch sizes.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>7B model:</strong> ~14GB at FP16, ~7GB at INT8, ~4GB at INT4 — fits an RTX 4090 (24GB) comfortably at any precision, or a T4 (16GB) at INT8/INT4.</li>
    <li><strong>13B model:</strong> ~26GB at FP16, ~13GB at INT8, ~7GB at INT4 — needs an L40S or A100 at FP16, or fits an RTX 4090 once quantized.</li>
    <li><strong>34B model:</strong> ~68GB at FP16, ~34GB at INT8, ~17GB at INT4 — typically needs an A100 80GB at FP16, or a single L40S/RTX 4090 once quantized to INT4.</li>
    <li><strong>70B model:</strong> ~140GB at FP16 (multiple 80GB GPUs with tensor parallelism), ~70GB at INT8 (fits one A100 80GB), ~35-40GB at INT4 (fits one L40S or A100 40GB).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Quantization trades a small amount of output quality for a large VRAM
    reduction — INT8 is usually close to lossless for most tasks, while INT4
    is more noticeable on tasks that need precise reasoning. It's often the
    difference between needing one GPU and needing four.
  </p>

  <h2>Real-world GPU prices vary far more than you'd expect</h2>
  <p>
    The prices in this calculator are rough, provider-agnostic approximations
    — actual cloud GPU pricing spreads widely depending on where you rent
    from. Budget-focused providers (RunPod, Vast.ai, Lambda Labs) routinely
    price the same card at 40-60% less than the major hyperscalers (AWS,
    Google Cloud, Azure), and spot/preemptible instances can undercut
    on-demand pricing by another 50-70% — at the cost of your instance being
    reclaimed with little warning. Always check current on-demand pricing
    from your actual provider before committing to a number from any
    calculator, this one included.
  </p>
  <p>
    Once you've settled on infrastructure cost, plan your actual usage
    against a budget with the <a href="/tools/token-budget-planner">Token Budget Planner</a>, or
    estimate fine-tuning cost separately with the <a href="/tools/fine-tune-cost-estimator">Fine-Tuning Cost Estimator</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>GPU Self-Hosting Cost FAQ</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>When does self-hosting make sense?</strong> Generally at high,
    predictable, sustained request volume — where the fixed GPU cost is
    lower than the equivalent pay-per-token API cost. At low or spiky
    volume, API pricing is usually cheaper since you don't pay for idle time.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Does this include setup and engineering time?</strong> No — this
    is infrastructure cost only. Self-hosting also requires DevOps effort for
    deployment, scaling, and monitoring, which has its own cost.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What GPU do I need to run a 70B model?</strong> At full FP16
    precision, expect to need multiple 80GB GPUs (like the A100 80GB or H100
    80GB) split across tensor parallelism. Quantized to INT4, a 70B model can
    fit on a single 40-48GB card, at some cost to output precision.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Is self-hosting Llama cheaper than the OpenAI or Claude API?</strong>
    Only at high, sustained volume — compare your actual token volume through
    the <a href="/tools/api-pricing-calculator">API Pricing Calculator</a> against the total this calculator gives you for the same time period. At
    low or unpredictable volume, the API is almost always cheaper once you
    account for GPU idle time.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's the cheapest way to rent a GPU?</strong> Budget-focused
    providers and spot/preemptible instances are typically far cheaper than
    on-demand pricing from major cloud providers — but spot instances can be
    reclaimed with little notice, so they suit interruptible batch work
    better than a production inference endpoint.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Does quantization hurt output quality?</strong> INT8 is usually
    close to indistinguishable from FP16 for most tasks. INT4 saves
    significantly more VRAM but shows more noticeable quality loss,
    particularly on tasks requiring precise multi-step reasoning.
  </p>
</ToolLayout>
